## Summary

`exhaustive-deps` treats the dispatch/setter returned by `useState`/`useReducer`/`useActionState` as a stable value, so you don't have to list it in a dependency array. But the stability check only fired when the hook result was destructured into exactly two elements. `useActionState` returns three: `[state, dispatch, isPending]`. As soon as you destructure the `isPending` element, the rule stopped recognizing `dispatch` as stable and reported a false positive:

```jsx
const [state, dispatch, isPending] = useActionState(action, 0);
useEffect(() => {
  dispatch();
}, []); // ⚠️ React Hook useEffect has a missing dependency: 'dispatch'
```

The two-element form `[state, dispatch]` works fine, which is what made this easy to miss. Same false positive as #32062.

## Fix

Allow the three-element tuple form specifically for `useActionState` (`useState`/`useReducer` stay restricted to two). The existing branch logic already handles the rest correctly: `dispatch` is stable, while `state` and `isPending` stay dynamic and must still be listed.

## Test plan

Added valid cases for the `[state, dispatch, isPending]` form (both `useActionState` and `React.useActionState`) and an invalid case confirming `state`/`isPending` are still required. `jest` passes for the full `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` suite (3680 tests).

Preserve the RefValue source location when joining mixed ref types so validation errors point to the original ref access.

Before this change it used to show the incorrect location when using the rust compiler.

```rust
  2 |   const ref = useRef(null);
  3 |   const x = cond ? ref : ref.current;
> 4 |   return <Foo value={x} />;
    |                      ^ Cannot access ref value during render
  5 | }
  6 |
```

## Summary

Propagate errors returned while lowering block statements instead of discarding them and continuing with partially built HIR.

The original issue was triggered by the SWC path, where a TypeScript `this` pseudo-parameter remains in the AST but is omitted from `ScopeInfo`. Lowering rejects the AST parameter, but the function-body block wrapper previously swallowed that error and returned a partial function.

```ts
function Component() {
  useEffect(() => {
    const get = (): Val => {
      window.value = {
        count: 0,
        method(this: Val) {},
      };
      return window.value;
    };
    get().count++;
  }, []);
}
```

This could emit a partial transform with the assignment removed:

```js
function Component() {
  useEffect(() => {
    const get = () => {
      return window.value;
    };
    get().count++;
  }, []);
}
```

## How did you test this change?

Added a [source-level reproduction against the SWC adapter](https://github.com/wbinnssmith/swc/blob/114e9c55b2/crates/swc_ecma_react_compiler/src/tests/integration.rs#L1563-L1591) using the same case above.

- With the current lowering crate, the test fails because the adapter emits a partial program.
- With this change patched into the lowering dependency, the test passes because compilation bails out.
- `cargo test --manifest-path compiler/Cargo.toml -p react_compiler_lowering`

## Summary

Treat ReactDOM.browser() as a recoverable reason when a thenable rejects with it.

Fizz materializes the branded rejection into the existing browser bailout. Fiber, as the final renderer, consumes it as undefined during the retry, so it does not reach an Error Boundary. This applies to both use(thenable) and thenables rendered in a child position through the shared thenable trackers.

## Why only the rejection path?

browser() is useful as a renderer control signal, not as application data. In Fizz it abandons the nearest recoverable boundary. In Fiber, use(browser()) produces undefined and lets browser rendering continue.

That means an asynchronous decision to switch a subtree to client rendering can be expressed by rejecting with browser():

```js
use(
  promise.then(v => {
    if (cond) {
      throw browser();
    }
    return v;
  }),
);
```

If the condition is true, Fizz bails out while Fiber consumes the special rejection as undefined and continues rendering. A thenable that fulfills with browser() does not add a similarly useful Fiber behavior: as a rendered value it is only an empty slot, and browser-only behavior at a subtree boundary can already be expressed by an ordinary component using browser(). Supporting the fulfilled form would nevertheless require extra checks in the common fulfilled-thenable path and child reconciliation. Supporting only rejection captures the useful asynchronous control flow without adding work to those hot paths.

This is also analogous to abort(browser()): browser() is the reason to stop server rendering, rather than a value produced by rendering.

## Implementation

The recoverable protocol is shared from ReactFizzRecoverable rather than ReactFizzHooks because ReactFizzHooks already imports ReactFizzThenable. ReactFizzThenable must now create the same recoverable error for rejected thenables, so importing those helpers from ReactFizzHooks would introduce a ReactFizzHooks -> ReactFizzThenable -> ReactFizzHooks cycle. Keeping the protocol in a separate module lets direct use(browser()), rejected thenables, and abort/server handling share one representation. It also avoids wrapping every Fizz thenable unwrap in try/catch solely to translate browser() after it is thrown.

## How did you test this change?

- ReactDOMBrowser-test, ReactDOMFizzServer-test, ReactUse-test, and ReactHooksInspection-test: 250 tests passed
- ESLint on all changed files
- Flow for the dom-node renderer in CI mode
- Prettier and git diff --check
